g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\nRedJoker: 1, BlackJoker: 1, A: 2, K: 1, Q: 2, 10: 1, 9: 1, 8: 1\nValid Moves:\nsingle: ♦7\nsingle: ♣10\nsingle: ♠K\nsingle: ♠A\nsingle: ♥2\npass: []\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation: The landlord just played a single ♠6, which is a low card. I can beat this with any higher single card. My valid single cards are ♦7, ♣10, ♠K, ♠A, and ♥2. Since twos are high (just below jokers), and the red joker is still unplayed (and likely with an opponent or teammate), playing ♥2 is very strong—it\'s one of the highest singles possible and could prevent the landlord from easily reclaiming control.\n\nHowever, as a peasant, my goal isn\'t just to play big cards but to help my teammate win. My teammate has only 4 cards left and may be close to going out. If I play too aggressively, I might block their chance to play. But in this case, my teammate passed after the landlord’s ♠6, meaning they likely don’t have a small card to continue a sequence or a useful mid-range card. So they may be waiting for a high card lead or a bomb/rocket to take control.\n\nLooking at my hand: I have no bombs or rockets, and only singles. Playing a high card now might allow me to take control and potentially set up my teammate. Among the playable cards, ♦7 is barely above ♠6, so it\'s weak and could be easily beaten by the landlord who still has 6 cards. Higher cards like ♣10, ♠K, ♠A, or ♥2 are safer.\n\nBut saving ♥2 (the highest single) might be valuable later, especially if the landlord tries to push with a high card or bomb. Similarly, ♠A and ♠K are strong but not unbeatable.\n\nThe optimal strategy here is to play the minimal winning card—just high enough to beat ♠6 but preserve higher cards for later. That would be ♦7.\n\nWait—important: In Dou Dizhu, **suits do not matter**, only rank. So ♦7 beats ♠6 because 7 > 6. But 7 is still quite low. The landlord could easily have 8, 9, 10, J, Q, K, A, 2, or jokers.\n\nIf I play ♦7, the landlord can easily beat it with a low card like 8 or 9, maintaining their advantage. Whereas if I play a high card like ♠A or ♥2, I’m more likely to actually win the trick and gain the lead—which is valuable.\n\nAs a peasant, gaining the lead allows me to control the game flow and possibly play toward my teammate (e.g., play a card they can cover or avoid blocking their path).\n\nMoreover, my teammate passed after the landlord’s ♠6, suggesting they cannot or prefer not to play on that level. They might be holding high cards or bombs and waiting for a better opportunity.\n\nThus, taking control now with a strong card gives us a chance to disrupt the landlord.\n\nAmong the high cards, playing ♥2 is overkill—it\'s second only to the rocket. It should be saved unless necessary. ♠A is high but less dominant than 2. Still, 2 is rare and powerful.\n\nBest balance: Play ♠A. It beats ♠6, is less extreme than ♥2, and preserves the 2 for later when it might be needed to overtake a bomb.\n\nAlternatively, if I believe the landlord may try to run small sequences or singles, using ♠A now could be wasted if they just pass.\n\nBut looking at the unknown cards:  \n- There are two Aces left unknown — I hold one (♠A), so one other Ace remains unseen.  \n- Two Kings — I hold one (♠K).  \n- So many high cards remain: both jokers, two Aces (one mine), etc.\n\nGiven that, the landlord may well have a higher card than ♠A (like 2 or joker), so even if I play ♠A, they might still beat it.\n\nBut if I don’t play now, we risk letting the landlord keep leading low cards and cycling through cheap plays.\n\nAnother angle: cooperation. My teammate has only 4 cards. If they are about to go out, I should avoid playing a card that blocks them. But since they passed on a ♠6, they probably can’t respond to a higher single either—unless they have pairs or bombs.\n\nSo likely, they need to gain the lead themselves via a pass chain.\n\nTherefore, I should try to gain the lead and play something useful.\n\nBut here\'s a key insight: **I have no follow-up power**. After playing a single card, I’ll likely be beaten by a higher card from the landlord (who has 6 cards), and then they get to lead again. So there’s little benefit in playing a high card now unless it forces a rocket or bomb.\n\nBetter to play minimally and conserve high cards.\n\nSo re-evaluate: Play ♦7 — the smallest card that beats ♠6. Why?\n\n- It wins the trick for now.\n- If the landlord beats it with a higher card, they waste a valuable card.\n- Then my teammate (with only 4 cards) might get a better chance later when the landlord runs low.\n- I preserve ♠K, ♠A, and ♥2 for future tricks where I can lead or counter-bomb.\n\nYes — this is classic peasant strategy: let the landlord burn high cards while peasants survive with minimal responses.\n\nAlso, if I play ♦7 and landlord plays, say, 8, then teammate can stay silent, and I lose nothing. But if I play ♠A and landlord uses 2, then I’ve wasted a major card.\n\nSo best to underplay.\n\nFinal consideration: Can I pass? No — because I must beat the previous play (♠6) if I can. I have ♦7 which is higher, so passing is illegal.\n\nTherefore, I must play a single card higher than 6.\n\nMinimal winning move: ♦7.\n\nReason: To minimize commitment and preserve high cards for later, I should play the lowest card that wins the trick. ♦7 beats ♠6 and forces the landlord to use a higher card if they wish to take control, helping exhaust their hand. This conserves my strong cards (♠K, ♠A, ♥2) for critical moments, especially since my teammate is close to emptying their hand.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♦7"]}'}
